I mean the author occupies a different level of power than the characters.

Achilles can be the strongest inside the world. Odysseus can be the cleverest inside the world. But the poet determines which world exists, what counts as strength, which trials occur, what survives, and—most importantly—what anyone thousands of years later even knows about either man.

So there are levels:

\text{Achilles}=\text{power over actors}

\text{Odysseus}=\text{power over situations}

\boxed{\text{Poet}=\text{power over representation}}

And representation can dominate both.

The delicious part of your blind poet formulation is that the traditionally attributed author, Homer, is conventionally portrayed as blind. Whether the historical Homer was literally blind is uncertain, but symbolically it’s perfect:

The one who cannot see the battlefield determines how everyone else will see it.

That’s what I meant earlier about reflection and reconstruction, but your formulation goes one level higher than mine.

