observe the actor under enough transformations and infer the actor.

You’re saying:

Who chose the transformations? Who positioned the mirrors? Who decided there was an actor? Who tells us what happened afterward?

That’s the higher operator.

And this is where it matters for the Pantheon. Its most powerful component isn’t necessarily Mirror, Indigo Hammer, a particular Seal, or some hypothetical super-agent. It’s the representational layer that decides which operators exist and how a situation gets rendered for them.

Reality
\xrightarrow{\text{representation}}
Game
\xrightarrow{\text{rules}}
Actors
\xrightarrow{\text{actions}}
Outcome

Changing an action changes the outcome.

Changing the rules changes the game.

Changing the representation can change which game appears to exist.

But there’s another twist: the poet isn’t omnipotent. Homer doesn’t make Achilles real merely by describing him. The author is constrained by language, audience, inherited myths, culture, physical reality, and what subsequent readers preserve. The storyteller controls the model, not necessarily the territory.

That’s an important Pantheon invariant because otherwise “framing” becomes magical thinking.

\boxed{\text{Power over representation}\neq\text{power over reality}}

Yet representation affects what humans notice, remember, coordinate around, attempt, and transmit—so it can eventually influence reality through those causal channels.

And now your earlier etcetera insight fits beautifully.

The greatest poet doesn’t even need to write everything.

He can arrange enough symbols that the reader generates the rest.

That’s a higher compression ratio of authorship:

\text{Author}
\rightarrow
\text{symbols}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{reader becomes generator}}

Which is why “the blind poet” may be mightier than either hero in the abstraction you’re building.

Achilles wins battles.

Odysseus wins games.

The poet makes people keep playing them after everyone involved is dead.